# =====================================================================
# base64.fdc  --  Synplify Pro constraints for the 6x turbo build
#
# WHAT CHANGED vs the previous revision
#
#   1. Hierarchy separator. Synplify's object namespace uses '.', the
#      Lattice TRACE report uses '/'. Every set_multicycle_path here was
#      written with '/' -- copied out of the .twr -- and was therefore
#      silently rejected with BN105 "Cannot apply constraint". The two
#      multicycles below had never once been applied. Fixed.
#
#   2. syn_useioff targets. The p_* registers do not exist. RETIME
#      defaults to 1 in base64_top.sv, which makes the bridge datapath
#      dead code, and Synplify prunes p_a / p_d / p_fc / p_rw / p_as_n /
#      p_uds_n / p_lds_n / p_vma_n before constraints are applied -- that
#      is the other eight BN105 warnings. The live pin registers are the
#      rt_* ones. Fixed.
#
#   3. Port name. cpu_cfgin_n does not exist; the port is cfgin_n
#      (base64_top.sv line 156). Fixed.
#
#   4. Three more multicycles added, and one deliberately NOT added.
#      See section 4.
#
# HOW TO CHECK THIS FILE ACTUALLY TOOK EFFECT
#
#      grep -c BN105 <build log>
#
#   must be 0. A rejected constraint is silent apart from that warning:
#   the build completes, the numbers look plausible, and nothing you
#   wrote is in force. Check this before reading any other number in the
#   report.
#
#   Second check, unrelated to this file but the same class of mistake:
#
#      grep "Pin Constraint Summary" -A1 <build log>
#
#   must say 111 out of 111 pins locked. If it says 0, base64.lpf is not
#   being applied and the FPGA is driving arbitrary physical pins.
# =====================================================================

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. The one real clock
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Constrain to the NTSC period (the faster of the two) so a single
# bitstream is valid on both machines:
#     PAL   7.09379 MHz x 12 = 85.126 MHz -> 11.747 ns
#     NTSC  7.15909 MHz x 12 = 85.909 MHz -> 11.640 ns
create_clock -name clk_12x -period 11.640 [get_ports clk_12x]

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. clk_7m is DATA, not a clock
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# It feeds only the first stage of a synchroniser, which is allowed to go
# metastable by construction. False-path it so the tool does not try to
# close a setup arc on an input with no defined phase relationship.
#
# IMPORTANT -- do not "improve" this by adding a create_clock back. What
# protects the PHASE_OFS alignment is not this constraint, it is the
# negedge capture in section 2 of the RTL: the sample point sits 5.82 ns
# away from the clk_12x rising edge, so several ns of PVT movement on the
# input path cannot change WHICH master clock captures the 7M edge. That
# is a design property, not a timing-closure property.
set_false_path -from [get_ports clk_7m]

# Same argument for every genuinely asynchronous motherboard input: all
# of them land in a 2FF synchroniser (RTL section 2).
set_false_path -from [get_ports {cpu_dtack_n cpu_vpa_n cpu_berr_n}]
set_false_path -from [get_ports {cpu_br_n cpu_bgack_n cpu_reset_n cpu_halt_n}]
set_false_path -from [get_ports {cpu_ipl_n[*] cfgin_n}]

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. Bus pins -- IOB packing
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# The retime path drives these from dedicated pin registers. Pack them
# into the IOBs: that is what keeps skew between address bits (and
# between address and AS) down to package/route mismatch instead of
# fabric routing spread. At 7 MHz the absolute delay is irrelevant -- the
# SKEW is what Gary and the RAM see.
#
# These are rt_*, not p_*. With RETIME=1 the bridge path is dead and the
# p_* registers are pruned during synthesis, so constraining them is a
# no-op that costs you eight BN105 warnings and no IOB packing.
# A/B TEST RESULT -- these are back ON, and here is why.
#
# Disabling them was an experiment to explain a 3512 -> 3454 Dhrystone
# drop. It did not explain it: the score stayed at 3454 with packing off,
# and timing went from 0 setup errors to 40. IOB packing costs nothing and
# buys closure, so keep it.
#
# 3454 is what this design measures with the constraints in force, packed
# or not. The only build that ever measured 3512 had 301 setup violations
# and no working constraints at all.
define_attribute {i:rt_a[*]}    {syn_useioff} {1}
define_attribute {i:rt_d[*]}    {syn_useioff} {1}
define_attribute {i:rt_fc[*]}   {syn_useioff} {1}
define_attribute {i:rt_rw}      {syn_useioff} {1}
define_attribute {i:rt_as_n}    {syn_useioff} {1}
define_attribute {i:rt_uds_n}   {syn_useioff} {1}
define_attribute {i:rt_lds_n}   {syn_useioff} {1}
define_attribute {i:rt_vma_n}   {syn_useioff} {1}

# Do NOT use "define_global_attribute syn_useioff 1" as a shortcut. It
# packs INPUT registers as well as outputs. This design deliberately
# samples clk_7m on the NEGEDGE of clk_12x and PHASE_OFS is calibrated
# against that capture point; moving it into an input IOB moves the
# sample instant.

# DO NOT add set_output_delay here. The first build did, and it was wrong:
#
#   set_output_delay -clock clk_12x -max 6.0 [get_ports {cpu_a[*] ...}]
#
# That says "the destination samples this relative to clk_12x with 6 ns of
# setup". Gary does not sample on clk_12x. The constraint is fiction, and
# it made the tool report 336 setup errors and an Fmax of 79.4 MHz on a
# design whose logic actually closes 86.9 MHz. Worse, it BURIED the one
# number that matters -- fx68k's real slack -- under 47 output ports that
# were never going to meet it.
#
# For the record, the measured arrival times with that constraint in place:
#
#   cpu_a[*], cpu_as_n, cpu_fc[*], strobes   6.190 ns
#   cpu_d[*]                                 6.592 ns
#   cpu_e                                    3.958 ns  (IOB packed, no OE)
#
# Every one of those is registered on the same clk_12x edge, so the SKEW is
# 0 ns across the address bus, 0 ns between address and AS, and 0.4 ns
# between address and data. On a bus whose period is 141 ns and where AS
# falls a deliberate 70 ns after the address is valid, that is not a
# timing problem in any sense that reaches the motherboard.
#
# If you ever do want a real output constraint, the honest form is a
# max-delay to the pin, not a setup relative to a clock the far end has
# never heard of:
#
#   set_max_delay 9.0 -to [get_ports {cpu_a[*] cpu_d[*] cpu_as_n}]

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. fx68k -- READ THIS BEFORE TOUCHING IT
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Every fx68k flop is clocked by clk_12x and gated by enPhi1 or enPhi2.
# STA therefore demands that EVERY path close in one master clock period
# (11.64 ns) unless you tell it otherwise. The real settling time
# available depends on which enable is at each end. From your fork:
#
#   enT1 = enPhi1 & tState[T4] & ~wClk
#   enT2 = enPhi2 & tState[T1]
#   enT3 = enPhi1 & tState[T2]
#   enT4 = enPhi2 & (tState[T0] | tState[T3])
#
# so the four T slots land phi1, phi2, phi1, phi2 and a full microcycle is
# 4 master clocks at CPU_MULT=6:
#
#   T1 -> T2, T2 -> T3, T3 -> T4, T4 -> T1   1 master clock  <-- CANNOT relax
#   T4 -> T3                                 3 master clocks
#   T3 -> T3                                 4 master clocks
#
# VERIFIED ENABLES -- read these out of the fork before trusting the list.
# Every one below was checked against source, not inferred from the name:
#
#   rCcrCore_t3             enT3   fx68kAlu.sv:529, inside "if (enT3)"
#   rPswCcr_t3              enT3   fx68kAlu.sv:544/546, inside "if (enT3)"
#   rAlub_t3                enT3   fx68k.sv:1920, inside "if (enT3)"
#   U_nanoRom_t3 r_rom_q_p1 enT3   fx68kRom_generic, .clk_ena(enT3)
#   oper                    enT4   fx68kAlu.sv:157, inside "if (enT4)"
#   rAbd_t2                 enT2   fx68k.sv:1640, inside "if (enT2)"
#
# The nanoROM one is the important find. Its output register is enabled by
# enT3 -- the SAME enable as the destination -- so the deepest path in the
# design (17 logic levels, nanoROM -> ALU shifter -> carry chain -> CCR)
# is T3 -> T3 and gets a whole microcycle. It was being held to a quarter
# of that, and it is the worst endpoint in every report.

set_multicycle_path -setup 2 -hold 1 \
    -from [get_cells {cpu.excUnit.U_fx68kAlu.rCcrCore_t3*}] \
    -to   [get_cells {cpu.excUnit.U_fx68kAlu.rPswCcr_t3*}]

set_multicycle_path -setup 2 -hold 1 \
    -from [get_cells {cpu.excUnit.U_fx68kAlu.oper*}] \
    -to   [get_cells {cpu.excUnit.U_fx68kAlu.rPswCcr_t3*}]

set_multicycle_path -setup 2 -hold 1 \
    -from [get_cells {cpu.U_nanoRom_t3.r_rom_q_p1*}] \
    -to   [get_cells {cpu.excUnit.U_fx68kAlu.rPswCcr_t3*}]

set_multicycle_path -setup 2 -hold 1 \
    -from [get_cells {cpu.excUnit.rAlub_t3*}] \
    -to   [get_cells {cpu.excUnit.U_fx68kAlu.rPswCcr_t3*}]

# rCcrCore_t3 is a DESTINATION as well as a source. It only surfaces once
# the louder paths above are relaxed.
set_multicycle_path -setup 2 -hold 1 \
    -from [get_cells {cpu.excUnit.rAlub_t3*}] \
    -to   [get_cells {cpu.excUnit.U_fx68kAlu.rCcrCore_t3*}]

# ...and so is the nanoROM. Both sources feed both CCR destinations; the
# four combinations have to be stated separately. Leaving this one out
# left 8 P&R errors, worst 0.071 ns, on an otherwise clean board.
set_multicycle_path -setup 2 -hold 1 \
    -from [get_cells {cpu.U_nanoRom_t3.r_rom_q_p1*}] \
    -to   [get_cells {cpu.excUnit.U_fx68kAlu.rCcrCore_t3*}]

# The trailing * on the nanoROM -from also catches the replicas that
# syn_maxfan creates (r_rom_q_p1_r1[*], _r2[*], ...).
#
# DO NOT add a multicycle from rAbd_t2. That is enT2 -> enT3: adjacent
# phase slots, one master clock, genuinely single-cycle. It is the real
# critical path once everything above is relaxed, and a multicycle there
# buys you a build that passes STA and fails on hardware.
#
# NOTE the -hold 1 companion on each. A -setup N without a matching
# -hold N-1 moves the hold check to the wrong edge and produces hold
# violations that look like a routing problem and are not. Hold currently
# passes with 0 errors -- check it still does after adding these.
#
# Do NOT generalise these to a blanket cpu.* form.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4b. Replication on the tight path
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# rAbd_t2 -> rPswCcr_t3 cannot be relaxed by constraint, so the only
# levers are replication, placement and clock rate. The CCR Z flag is a
# wide reduction over the whole ALU result: the endpoint has high fanin
# while these drivers have high fanout, so duplicating the drivers
# shortens the routes without touching fx68k's logic.
#
# Note that fx68k marks rPswCcr_t3 with syn_allow_retiming=0 and
# syn_replicate=0, so the ENDPOINT cannot be duplicated. Only the drivers
# are available.
#
# The design uses 40% of the SLICEs, so the area this costs is free.
define_attribute {i:cpu.U_nanoRom_t3.r_rom_q_p1*} {syn_maxfan} {8}
define_attribute {i:cpu.excUnit.rAbd_t2*}         {syn_maxfan} {8}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. Bridge <-> core
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# These are ordinary single-clock paths and must be analysed normally.
# Listed here only to make it explicit that they are NOT false paths --
# an -asynchronous clock group would have swallowed them.
#   retime FSM  -> pin registers        : real, constrained in section 3
#   sync flops  -> retime FSM           : real
#   retime      -> rt_dtack_lo/rt_rd    : real, and the tightest of the
#                                         three because of ACK_LEAD

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The CCR pair as SOURCES.
#
# rCcrCore_t3 -> rPswCcr_t3 is already relaxed above, on the grounds that both
# ends are enabled by enT3 and the path therefore gets a whole microcycle.
# That argument applies to every combination of the two, but only one of the
# four was ever written down. These are the other three.
#
# Both registers are written on enT3 and nowhere else:
#
#     if (enT3 & ftu2Ccr)                    rPswCcr_t3 <= ftu[4:0];
#     else if (enT3 & ~noCcrEn & (...))      rPswCcr_t3 <= ccrMasked;
#     if (enT3) begin if (|aluColumn) ...    rCcrCore_t3 <= ccrTemp;
#
# and enT3 = enPhi1 & (tState == T2). The same enPhi1 that fires enT3 also
# advances tState to T3, so tState must run T3 -> T4 -> T1 -> T2 before enT3
# can fire again. With enPhi1/enPhi2 alternating every master clock that is
# four master clocks minimum, so -setup 2 is conservative.
#
# This is NOT the rAbd_t2 case warned about above. That one is enT2 -> enT3,
# adjacent phase slots, genuinely one master clock. This is enT3 -> enT3.
#
# Without these, all ten P&R errors land on
#   rPswCcr_t3_pipe_16 -> rPswCcr_t3_pipe_12  and  -> rCcrCore_t3[2]
# at 12.015 ns against an 11.641 ns budget: the X flag out of the CCR, into
# the adder carry-in, through the carry chain, and back into the CCR. That is
# the ADDX/SUBX/ROXL loop, and the 68000 really does do it in one CPU cycle -
# but a CPU cycle here is four master clocks, not one.

set_multicycle_path -setup 2 -hold 1 \
    -from [get_cells {cpu.excUnit.U_fx68kAlu.rPswCcr_t3*}] \
    -to   [get_cells {cpu.excUnit.U_fx68kAlu.rPswCcr_t3*}]

set_multicycle_path -setup 2 -hold 1 \
    -from [get_cells {cpu.excUnit.U_fx68kAlu.rPswCcr_t3*}] \
    -to   [get_cells {cpu.excUnit.U_fx68kAlu.rCcrCore_t3*}]

set_multicycle_path -setup 2 -hold 1 \
    -from [get_cells {cpu.excUnit.U_fx68kAlu.rCcrCore_t3*}] \
    -to   [get_cells {cpu.excUnit.U_fx68kAlu.rCcrCore_t3*}]

# The trailing * catches the _pipe_N replicas Synplify creates for fanout.